---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import RelatedGuides from '../../components/RelatedGuides.astro';
import CtaBand from '../../components/CtaBand.astro';
import { site } from '../../config/site';

const path = '/guides/how-hwid-bans-work';
const title = 'How HWID Bans Work';
const description =
  'The general model behind a hardware ban: device identifiers, stored penalties, and why a new account often changes nothing.';
const crumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
  { name: 'Guides', path: '/guides' },
  { name: title, path },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} breadcrumbs={crumbs} article>
  <article class="shell py-12 sm:py-16" data-reveal>
    <Breadcrumbs items={crumbs} />
    <header class="mt-8 max-w-3xl">
      <p class="text-xs font-medium tracking-[0.18em] text-accent uppercase">Guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-5xl sm:text-6xl">{title}</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 text-xl text-muted">
        A hardware ban is a stored match between a penalty and a fingerprint of the PC. This page
        stays with that model. It does not pretend to know unpublished anti-cheat formulas.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-sm text-muted">Updated {site.updatedLabel}</p>
    </header>

    <div class="article-body mt-10">
      <h2>A useful model</h2>
      <p>
        Think of three steps. Something on the PC collects identifiers. Something on the server
        stores a ban against a fingerprint derived from them. A later session is checked against
        that record. If the machine still looks like the banned fingerprint, the session can be
        refused even though the account is new.
      </p>
      <p>
        That is the whole idea behind an <a href="/guides/what-is-an-hwid-ban">HWID ban</a>. The
        interesting limits sit around the edges: which identifiers, how stable they are, and how
        often the publisher changes the check. Those details are not public, they differ by game,
        and they move. Anyone selling a permanent, universal method is guessing.
      </p>

      <h2>What a hardware ID is made from</h2>
      <p>
        In ordinary discussion, a hardware ID draws on components and firmware rather than on your
        password or your email. Examples that come up repeatedly are storage devices, the
        motherboard, the network adapter’s hardware address, and system firmware values. Graphics
        hardware and other peripherals are mentioned in some cases. A game may use several of these
        and ignore others.
      </p>
      <p>
        Two consequences follow. First, replacing one part, or editing one setting, often leaves
        the rest of the fingerprint intact. Second, a reinstall of Windows does not automatically
        create a new hardware ID, because many of the inputs live below the operating system’s
        user accounts.
      </p>

      <h2>How a ban gets stored</h2>
      <p>
        The ban record is not a sticker on your PC that you can peel off locally. The publisher
        keeps it. Your machine presents identifiers at launch. Their servers decide whether that
        presentation matches a banned record. Cleaning game files, making a new launcher account,
        or switching Wi-Fi networks does not delete their record.
      </p>
      <p>
        A hardware ID ban is stored against the machine. The
        <a href="/games">game pages</a> describe how that shows up in each title.
      </p>

      <h2>Why a new account often fails</h2>
      <p>
        A new account changes the login. It does not change the disk, the board, or the other
        identifiers the fingerprint was built from. If the check is really on the hardware ID, the
        new account walks into the same comparison and fails it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Failure can be immediate, or it can show up after a match, a delay, or a later security
        sweep. Speed is not proof by itself. A better comparison is whether a trusted older account
        on that same PC is also blocked, and whether the new account works on a different PC. Those
        two results point in opposite directions.
      </p>

      <h2>Why changing one identifier often fails</h2>
      <p>
        People try a new network adapter address, a VPN, or a single registry edit because those
        are easy to find. None of those present a full new hardware profile. A single identifier
        change leaves every other signal alone. If the fingerprint uses more than one signal, the
        machine can still match.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is a second problem. Anti-cheat updates add, drop, and reweight signals. A change
        that lined up with last year’s check can be irrelevant after a patch, or it can itself look
        inconsistent enough to draw attention. That is a reason to be skeptical of step-by-step
        lists copied from old threads.
      </p>

      <h2>What “undetected” refers to</h2>
      <p>
        An HWID spoofer presents a different hardware ID than the one stored with the ban. Players
        look for that when the account swap has already failed. “Undetected” describes a narrower
        claim: the spoofer is not currently flagged, on its own, by the anti-cheat on the games it
        supports. It does not mean the publisher has approved it. It does not mean a future update
        will ignore it. It does not mean every existing ban will open.
      </p>
      <p>
        The product linked from this site is positioned that way for the supported titles, mainly
        where hardware bans are the problem being solved. Detection status belongs on the product
        page, because it can change without this article changing.
      </p>

      <h2>What this guide does not claim</h2>
      <p>
        It does not name the current identifier list for Ricochet, Vanguard, BattlEye, Easy
        Anti-Cheat, or any other product. Those vendors do not publish it, and a copied list would
        go stale. It also does not give a procedure for altering identifiers by hand. A partial
        change is how people make a machine look inconsistent, which is a poor trade for a maybe.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you want the decision in one line: a spoofer is for the ban that follows the computer.
        Game terms usually forbid that. Further publisher penalties are possible.
      </p>
      <RelatedGuides current={path} />
    </div>
  </article>
  <CtaBand />
</BaseLayout>
